    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "In the past two decades, South Korea has experienced a shift from a homogenous to an ethnically diverse population largely due to an influx of migrants. When children born into these families enroll in Korean public schools, they alter classroom dynamics and demonstrate the need for better adaptation. Although a multicultural curriculum has been developed, the strong emphasis on the Korean language and culture has further led to maladjustment and marginalization. Our study explores the association between the implemented multicultural curriculum and the well-being of students from diverse backgrounds. We conducted semistructured interviews with students and teachers to gather accounts of their experiences in schools. Our findings identify components of well-being such as positive relationships, a sense of belonging, strong language skills, and a character development process that influences identity formation and school adjustment. We argue that the current multicultural curriculum is superficially implemented and perceived as inadequate for effective integration.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Kim, D. (2024). Voices of Students and Teachers: Multicultural Education and the Well-being of Students from Diverse Backgrounds in South Korea. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/gj900ht6464. https://doi.org/10.25740/gj900ht6464.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
